# Phase 1 — Gmail read-only (plain English)

## What was built

The app can now **read** your Gmail. That's the only new power. Nothing about your email changes.

From your point of view, the new things are:

- A **Connect Gmail** button on the app's homepage.
- A Google consent screen that shows up when you click it — Google's own screen, not ours — listing every permission the app is asking for.
- A **preview page** that shows your last 10 messages (sender, subject, date, one-line snippet) as proof the connection works.
- A **Disconnect** button that cleanly removes the app's access.

The app can also read your Google Contacts and "Other Contacts" (people you email regularly but haven't formally saved). This is groundwork for a later safety rule that says: *don't hide email from people you actually talk to.*

## Why this matters

Everything the agent will eventually do — classify, organize, digest, alert — starts with "the app needs permission to look at your inbox". This phase sets that permission up in the safest possible way:

1. It asks for the **smallest** set of Google permissions that later phases still need.
2. It **never** asks for permission to send, modify, archive, or delete email. Google itself will block those actions even if a bug tried them.
3. The permission Google gives back is stored **encrypted** on your own computer, unreadable to anyone who doesn't also have your app's secret key.
4. The connection uses a signed handshake so a shady website can't trick your browser into finishing the connection on their behalf.

> **Definitions:** *"OAuth"* is Google's system for granting an app one specific permission on your account without ever sharing your password. A *"scope"* is one specific permission (like "read Gmail"). A *"refresh token"* is the long-lived pass the app stores after you consent — kept encrypted here.

## Exact Gmail permissions requested

These are the **only** permissions the app asks for. The consent screen will show the same list.

| Permission | What it lets the app do |
|---|---|
| Sign-in | Confirm which Google account you approved. |
| Email address | See which Gmail address is connected. |
| **Read Gmail** | Read message headers, bodies, labels, threads. **Cannot send, modify, or delete.** |
| Read Contacts | See people you've saved as Google Contacts. |
| Read Other Contacts | See people you've emailed but haven't formally saved. |

## What it can and can't change in Gmail

- **Can:** read message headers, message bodies, labels, threads, contacts.
- **Cannot:** send, reply, forward, archive, delete, trash, mark as read/unread, mark as spam, add labels, remove labels, change settings, or make **any** modification.

That's a hard limit of the "read Gmail" permission — Google itself rejects any write attempt, even if the app's code tried.

## How you'd notice it worked

- Before you connect: the homepage says "Not connected yet" and lists the exact permissions the app is about to request.
- You click **Connect Gmail** and land on Google's own consent screen. It should show the same permission list as the homepage.
- After you approve: the homepage now says "Connected as your.email@gmail.com".
- Clicking **Preview last 10 messages** shows real message headers from your inbox (sender, subject, date, snippet).
- Clicking **Disconnect** wipes the stored permission and returns the homepage to "Not connected yet".

## How to undo it

Phase 1 changes nothing on Google's side except granting a permission that *you* can revoke at any time. To fully undo:

1. In the app, click **Disconnect** — this deletes the stored permission on your computer.
2. Go to https://myaccount.google.com/permissions and revoke the "Gmail Intelligence Agent" grant on Google's side too.
3. Nothing in your Gmail changed. The app couldn't have changed anything even if it wanted to.

## What success looks like

- Homepage shows the connect button and the full permission list before you connect.
- Google's consent screen lists the same five permissions and nothing else.
- After consent, the homepage shows "Connected as \<your email>".
- The preview page shows real message headers.
- Disconnect fully removes access.

## What's next

**Phase 2 — Sheets control store.** The app will auto-create a Google Sheets workbook that becomes your editable "control panel": rules, VIPs, learned suggestions, audit log, deadlines, etc. Still no Gmail modifications.

---

## Running it yourself

You need three things ready before you start:

- **A Google Cloud OAuth client** — the ID and secret Google gave you when you set up the project on their side. The client's "authorized redirect URL" must be exactly `http://localhost:8000/oauth/callback`, and your Gmail address must be listed as a "test user" on Google's consent screen settings.
- **A random secret** the app uses to encrypt the stored Google permission. Generate one with:
  ```powershell
  py -3.13 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
  ```

Then, in PowerShell from the project folder:

1. **Copy the example config file** (only needed the first time).
   ```powershell
   Copy-Item .env.example .env -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
   ```

2. **Open `.env` in Notepad and fill in three values**: your Google client ID, your Google client secret, and the random secret from above.
   ```powershell
   notepad .env
   ```

3. **Activate the Python sandbox** built in Phase 0.
   ```powershell
   .\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
   ```

4. **Run the self-check** — should end with a "passed" line.
   ```powershell
   pytest
   ```

5. **Start the app.**
   ```powershell
   uvicorn app.main:app --reload --port 8000
   ```

Then open `http://localhost:8000/` in your browser and click **Connect Gmail**.

If Google's consent screen shows "This app isn't verified" — that's normal for a personal project. Add your Gmail address as a test user in Google's OAuth consent screen settings, or click "Advanced → Go to app (unsafe)". It's safe because *you* built the app; the warning exists to protect people from unknown developers.

If Google says "redirect_uri_mismatch", the redirect URL saved in Google Cloud doesn't match `http://localhost:8000/oauth/callback` exactly. Fix it in Google Cloud → Credentials → your OAuth client.
